{
  "rationale": "The submitted solution counts lines that separate two points, which equals the minimum steps. Correct answers must satisfy: boundedness (0 ≤ answer ≤ n), integer output, symmetry in swapping points, invariance under line reordering (reverse, sort), and geometric transformations preserving sidedness (negating coefficients, reflecting axes, swapping coordinates, rotating 90°, negating points and c). These follow from the problem definition—the count depends only on the set of lines and relative positions, not on order or coordinate choices. Each test checks one property; a violation indicates a wrong answer. The bounds test is a basic safety net; symmetry and invariance tests catch backdoors that break geometric consistency. Transformations include validity checks to ensure points remain off lines, skipping otherwise. The first to drop if limited would be rotate_90, as it is less obvious and may skip more inputs.",
  "plan": [
    {
      "name": "test_bounds",
      "intent": "Assert that the output is an integer between 0 and n (inclusive). A violation is an output outside this range."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_integer",
      "intent": "Assert that the output consists of a single integer with no extra whitespace or characters. A violation is non-integer output."
    },
    {
