{
  "rationale": "The tests focus on properties derived from the specification: output format invariance, symmetry under relabeling of rooms/switches, independence of isolated components, and a special case where the answer is known (all doors initially unlocked must yield YES). These catch errors that might depend on ordering, mishandling of zero-control switches, or incorrect consistency checks. The duplicate-system test verifies that disjoint systems do not interfere. The first test to drop would be test_all_unlocked, as it only covers a specific case, while the others are general metamorphic relations.",
  "plan": [
    {
      "name": "test_invariant_output",
      "intent": "The output must be exactly the string 'YES' or 'NO', with no extra characters or whitespace. Any other output is incorrect."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_isolated_switch",
      "intent": "Adding a switch that controls no rooms must not change the answer. Modify the input by incrementing m by 1 and appending a line '0'. The output on the modified input must equal the original output."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_permute_switches",
      "intent": "Randomly permuting the order of the m switch lines (keeping the first two lines unchanged) must not change the answer. Construct the permuted input and assert the output matches the original."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_permute_rooms",
      "intent": "Randomly permuting the room numbers 1..n (adjusting the initial door states list and all room references in switch lines accordingly) must not change the answer. Construct the permuted input and assert the output matches the original."
    },
